.TH ORBIS 1 "2026-09-09" "Orbis 0.1.0-beta.1.dev.9" "Orbis manual"
.SH NAME
orbis \- your Linux software, in one place
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B orbis
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Orbis is a beginner-friendly Linux interface for finding, understanding, updating, and safely managing software. It keeps provider-specific details available for advanced users while using natural language in the normal interface.
.PP
The public Beta 1 release is safety-first and still evolving. Review every plan before allowing package state to change.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Emit structured JSON instead of terminal presentation.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI styling.
.TP
.B --plain
Use deterministic plain output without animation or cursor movement.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print help.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the Orbis version.
.SH COMMON COMMANDS
.TP
.B find \fIQUERY\fR
Find software across supported systems.
.TP
.B show \fIPACKAGE\fR
Learn what software does, whether it is installed, and where it came from.
.TP
.B install \fIPACKAGE\fR
Review and install one exact piece of software.
.TP
.B remove \fIPACKAGE\fR
Review and remove one exact piece of software without purge or autoremove.
.TP
.B update
Check for available software updates. Read-only by default.
.TP
.B refresh
Refresh software information without upgrading installed software.
.TP
.B clean
Review confidently unused software items for safe cleanup.
.TP
.B history
Read previous Orbis operations.
.TP
.B health
Check that software systems are working.
.TP
.B self-update
Check for and safely install a newer Orbis release. Development builds never replace themselves.
.TP
.B ui, dashboard
Open the optional persistent full-screen interface. Ordinary commands run inline and return to the shell.
.SH UPDATE OPTIONS
.TP
.B update --plan
Build and show a read-only plan for applying available updates.
.TP
.B update --apply
Review, confirm, execute, verify, and record available updates.
.TP
.B update --yes
Apply the exact reviewed update plan without a second confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B refresh --plan
Show refresh work without changing software information.
.TP
.B upgrade
Compatibility command for applying a reviewed update plan.
.TP
.B self-update --check
Check the official GitHub release channel without downloading or replacing anything.
.SH ADVANCED COMMANDS
.TP
.B sources
Inspect exact software systems, backends, capabilities, and limitations.
.TP
.B why \fIPACKAGE\fR
Inspect evidence for why an installed package is present.
.TP
.B search, info, explain, updates, doctor
Compatibility spellings for existing scripts and advanced workflows.
.SH SAFETY
.B --plan
and
.B --dry-run
stop before execution. Without
.B --yes,
an actual mutating operation requires an interactive terminal and explicit confirmation. Ambiguous software names are never silently selected; use an interactive source chooser or provide
.B --source
or a qualified reference. Administrator authorization is requested only for the exact reviewed system operation.
.PP
Transient operations show real lifecycle and provider activity in bounded inline output. The explicit full-screen interface may show a persistent details view. They do not invent percentages. Piped, non-TTY, JSON, and
.B --plain
output remains deterministic and contains no terminal control sequences.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/orbis/transactions/
Sanitized transaction records.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/state/orbis/transactions/
Fallback transaction-record directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt-get (8),
.BR flatpak (1),
.BR snap (8)
